{% extends 'landing/base.html.twig' %}

{#
   Section order follows design/Main.dc.html (the landing redesign spec, published
   at https://claude.ai/artifact/HY4WFVivm8fxEjbnwaEqg5), top to bottom:

     header → #top → #telemetry → #pillars → #spotlight → #role → #journey
     → #facilities → #dressing-room → #leagues → #scouting → no-pay-to-win band
     → #owner-spotlight → #testers → final CTA → footer

   Two sections used to be nested children and are now top level, which is the
   substantive structural change in this pass:

     - _telemetry was _chairmans_terminal, a widget inside _hero. The redesign
       gives the feed its own full-bleed terminal with a pause control and real
       per-line buttons, which does not fit beside hero copy.
     - _owner_spotlight was included from _story (then Journey Chapter 01, as the
       "here are the owners who did this" payoff). It is now its own section late
       in the page, directly after the no-pay-to-win band, where the Rich List
       reads as a leaderboard rather than as chapter furniture.

   The redesign also drops thirteen sections that used to live here: the feature
   marquee (_ticker), the screenshot gallery (_screens) and its _shot macro,
   _stats_band, _features, _excursions, _archetypes, _depth, _tick, _consensus,
   _video, _community and _leaderboards. Those files are deliberately left on
   disk rather than deleted — the design replaces the page's whole imagery
   strategy (sprites, no screenshots), and restoring any one of them is a single
   include line. Nothing else references them; _shot is now used only by the
   orphaned _screens.

   _period_awards (#period-awards, after #owner-spotlight) is a LATER addition and
   is NOT the orphaned _leaderboards coming back. _leaderboards is a 12-tab,
   JS-fetched widget over /api/leaderboard/{category} — current standings, live.
   _period_awards is who WON a period that has ended: frozen at close,
   server-rendered, no API endpoint. Restoring _leaderboards is still a one-line
   include and the two do not conflict — but anyone restoring it expecting the
   awards feature has the wrong file.
#}

